debates, formatting strategy documents and organizing bullet points.

107
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:57,680
It's grueling work.

108
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:03,280
But instead, they fed the raw transcripts of highly complex, nuanced human debates directly

109
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:04,400
to the AI.

110
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,640
The agents handled the menial drafting of the strategy document.

111
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:11,240
Which frees up the human experts to actually do the hard part.

112
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:12,240
Exactly.

113
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:16,320
Because they weren't exhausted from typing, they spent their cognitive energy on high level

114
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:17,680
synthesis.

115
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:22,320
They used the AI to test the political feasibility of their strategy.

116
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:27,680
They essentially argued with the machine about policy nuance instead of formatting a word

117
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:28,680
document.

118
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:33,280
And that works beautifully when you have a structured team and a clear delegation of tasks

119
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:35,680
between human and machine.

120
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:40,720
But that fear of losing control that you mentioned earlier, it often drives people in the exact

121
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:41,720
opposite direction.

122
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:43,400
They don't elevate to the control tower.

123
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:45,680
They try to do everything themselves.

124
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:49,800
This brings us to the solar flight fallacy found in the software development sources.

125
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:50,800
Ah, right.

126
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:55,840
The idea that because I have AI, I don't need my team anymore, I can be a one person unicorn

127
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:56,840
