154
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:20,920
And the problem with that final 5% is that LLMs are probabilistic engines.

155
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:25,480
They basically predict the next most likely token based on their training data.

156
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:30,080
They are incredibly good at the standard 95% path because there is massive amounts of data

157
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:31,080
for it.

158
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:38,040
Edge cases require deep contextual, specific understanding that models just lack.

159
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:42,760
So if you are micro managing the AI and trying to fly solo, solving that last 5% requires

160
00:07:42,760 --> 00:07:46,600
you to painstakingly reverse engineer the AI's logic.

161
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:47,600
Exactly.

162
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:51,040
It will take you days and you completely wipe out all the efficiency gains you made in

163
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:52,040
the first 95%.

164
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:55,920
So if I grip the steering wheel too tightly, I end up crashing anyway because I just become

165
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:00,520
a glorified, exhausted editor of slightly wrong AI outputs.

166
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:04,040
The assembly line is moving too fast for me to check every single widget.

167
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:08,200
The solution is to structurally enforce the idea that the AI is an assembly line not a

168
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:10,000
free thinking colleague.

169
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,080
But in here is the catch, if we successfully step back and let the automated assembly line

170
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:18,440
run, we introduce a new, arguably more insidious psychological threat.

171
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:19,440
Cognitive atrophy.

172
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:23,920
If the machine is doing the drafting, the organizing and the coding, our brains are going to get

173
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:24,920
soft.

174
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:27,240
The Brookings paper warns about this explicitly.

175
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:32,680
If we rely entirely on AI to do the heavy lifting of thinking and structuring arguments,

176
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:37,520
human teams, particularly junior staff who are still learning their craft, might lose

177
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:39,200
their ability to think critically.

178
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:40,200
Which makes sense.

179
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:41,200
Yeah.

180
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:44,560
If you never struggle through writing a bad first draft, how do you develop the critical

181
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:46,440
eye to know what a good draft looks like?

182
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:47,840
You don't.

183
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:50,480
I look at the photography world for a parallel here.

184
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:55,680
Back in the day, manually exposing a photograph and getting the focus perfectly sharp was a highly

185
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:57,000
technical skill.

